  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Exploring New Battle Scenarios**\n\nI\'m currently focused on how `NewBattleModal` handles situations with a limited number of bladers. I\'m implementing logic to account for cases where fewer than two bladers are present. This will provide users with flexible options: entering quick names directly or selecting from pre-registered bladers for a seamless experience.\n\n\n**Refining Data Structures**\n\nI\'ve been examining `src/data/initialData.ts` and made some crucial updates. I\'ve decided to maintain the comprehensive `INITIAL_BEYBLADE_PARTS` for tournament references. I\'ve also set `INITIAL_BLADERS` and `INITIAL_MATCHES` to empty arrays, ensuring a clean slate for user-created data. I am also planning to maintain tournament settings. The current state of `App.tsx` is being reviewed next.\n\n\n**Designing Dashboard Layout**\n\nI am now focusing on the `DashboardScreen`\'s layout and functionality. It will feature a dynamic display based on whether a `currentMatch` exists. If there\'s an ongoing match, it will display the live console. Otherwise, it renders a tactical arena console with options to start a new match. It will also allow rapid match initiation with or without registered bladers, with some thoughtful UI for both.\n\n\n**Implementing Empty States**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the user experience for when data is absent. I\'m building out empty states for several screens: \"Sin enfrentamientos en espera\" in the queue, \"No hay enfrentamientos programados\" in `MatchesScreen`, and a similar message for the blader history and registration sections. I\'m focusing on providing clear calls to action, such as buttons to schedule new matches or generate tournament brackets. I\'m also ensuring the bracket screen visually indicates an empty state, prompting bracket generation upon blader registration, avoiding hardcoded examples.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 5
      nanos: 895754600
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
