    thinking: "**Identifying Branding Components**\n\nI\'ve started identifying the components and files showcasing the community\'s branding. I\'ve pinpointed `types.ts`, specifically the `TournamentConfig` interface, where fields like `leagueName` (e.g., \"Comunidad Beyblade C\303\272cuta\") and a potential `communityTagline` or `badgeText` are crucial. Next, I\'ll examine the usage of these properties throughout the project.\n\n\n**Defining Configuration Data**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the community-specific branding details should be structured within the configuration. I\'ve broken down essential elements like the badge text (e.g., \"C\303\232CUTA \342\200\242 OFICIAL\"), community city, organizer name, logo URL, tournament name, season, and arena status. I\'m exploring default, clean values in `initialData.ts` and considering adding `community_config` or `tournament_config` tables to `supabase.ts`. Implementing functions to sync and fetch configuration data to Supabase and local storage ensures changes are persisted efficiently.\n\n\n**Refining Visual Representation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on UI elements. I need to make the logo dynamic in `CommunityLogo.tsx`, displaying either a custom image or a dynamic SVG based on the community name and city. The `Sidebar.tsx` requires integrating `leagueName` and `logoUrl` with logo upload capabilities. In `Header.tsx`, I\'ll replace hardcoded values with dynamic `leagueName`, `tagline`, and `city` data from the configuration. Finally, I\'ll add an \"Identity & Multitenancy\" section in `ConfigScreen.tsx`.\n\n\n"
